A real-time clock (RTC) is an electronic component that maintains time and calendar information independently of a host processor's main operating state. Many RTC designs use a backup power source to preserve time when the main system is switched off or loses power. This functionality is important for timestamping, scheduled wake-up operations, event logging and systems that cannot rely continuously on network-based time synchronization.

